INT. APARTMENT - KITCHEN - NIGHT

A small kitchen. One lamp. A kettle gone cold on the stove. A phone propped against a bowl of fruit, its screen dark.

IRIS LENNOX, 30s, sits at the table in a cardigan, a mug between her hands, a letter folded beside it. She stares at the phone.

She picks it up. Puts it down. Picks it up again. Thumbs the screen. Holds it to her ear.

She unfolds the letter and reads the first line under her breath.

IRIS (V.O.)
(reading, as if remembered)
"Dear Iris. I have started this letter nine times."

It rings, tinnily, three times. Four. Then a flat electronic beep, no greeting at all.

IRIS
Hi. It's me.

She stops. Closes her eyes.

IRIS (CONT'D)
That's a stupid way to start. You know it's me. You've always known.

She laughs, once, and wipes her eye.

IRIS (CONT'D)
I got your letter. I read it standing up in the hallway because I couldn't make it to the kitchen. I read it four times. It's a very good letter. You always did write better than you talked.

She turns the letter over.

IRIS (CONT'D)
You said you were sorry. You said it three times, actually. I counted. Sorry for leaving. Sorry for the way you left. Sorry for the Thursday.

A long pause. Her thumb moves along the mug's rim.

IRIS (CONT'D)
I want to tell you I'm not angry. That would be easier. But I am a little. Not at you. At the Thursday. At whoever decided Thursday was a good day for a person to decide something like that.

She sets the phone on the table, on speaker, and leans toward it.

IRIS (CONT'D)
You asked if I'd call. I said I wouldn't. I've been sitting here for forty minutes not calling. So.

She drinks the cold tea. Grimaces.

IRIS (CONT'D)
Here's what I wanted to say. I wanted to say that the dog is fine. He's fat. He misses you. He sits by the door at six. I know that's not fair to tell you but I'm telling you.

The line hisses softly.

IRIS (CONT'D)
And I wanted to say that I forgive you, but I'm not ready to say it out loud, so I'm saying it into a machine, which is a little like a church, if you think about it. Nobody answers but you feel lighter.

She laughs again, wetter.

IRIS (CONT'D)
I'm going to hang up now. Or you'll have to listen to all the rest. There's a lot of rest.

She reaches for the screen, then pulls her hand back.

IRIS (CONT'D)
Daniel. Call me on Sunday. Or don't. But the porch light will be on.

She ends the call. The kitchen is very quiet. Slowly, deliberately, she gets up, goes to the window, and flicks on the porch light.
